      "hints": [
        "Substitute $x = 4y - c$ into $y^2 = x + 3$ to get $y^2 - 4y + (c - 3) = 0$.",
        "Set discriminant $\\Delta = (-4)^2 - 4(1)(c - 3) = 0$ for tangency and solve for $c$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Rearrange the line equation to express $x$ in terms of $y$:\n$$4y = x + c \\implies x = 4y - c$$",
        "Substitute into the curve equation $y^2 = x + 3$:\n$$y^2 = (4y - c) + 3 \\implies y^2 - 4y + (c - 3) = 0$$",
        "Apply the tangency condition requiring discriminant $\\Delta = 0$:\n$$(-4)^2 - 4(1)(c - 3) = 0 \\implies 16 - 4c + 12 = 0 \\implies 28 - 4c = 0$$\n$$4c = 28 \\implies c = 7$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
